    "content": "The Federal Trade Commission (FTC) is an independent U.S. government agency established in 1914 to promote competition and protect consumers by preventing unfair, deceptive, or anticompetitive business practices. Through law enforcement, rulemaking, and education, the FTC regulates advertising, privacy, antitrust issues, and fraud. Key Aspects of the FTC: Mission: To protect consumers from fraud/deception and promote competition by enforcing antitrust laws. Structure: Composed of five Commissioners appointed by the President for 7-year terms, with no more than three from the same political party. Core Bureaus: Bureau of Consumer Protection: Prevents fraudulent and deceptive practices. Bureau of Competition: Enforces antitrust laws to maintain competitive markets. Bureau of Economics: Analyzes the impact of FTC actions on the economy. Actions: Files lawsuits against companies, issues regulations (e.g., Franchise Rule), and provides educational resources. Reporting: Consumers can report fraud, scams, or unfair business practices at ReportFraud.ftc.gov. The FTC frequently targets unlawful charges, deceptive advertising, and anticompetitive mergers to ensure fair marketplaces.",
